WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.290
Movies have to be particularly careful when
it comes to adding controversial scenes.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.029
Since everything's right there on the screen,
there isn't much left to the imagination.

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:11.100
Books, on the other hand, can get away with
a lot more, which is why adapting a book to

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.800
film can be tricky business.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.640
So which film adaptations left the most controversial
scenes on the cutting room floor?

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.780
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.700
It: Chapter One

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.840
Easily the most controversial scene in Stephen
King's 1986 novel It takes place after the

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.770
Losers' Club defeats the evil entity in the
sewers.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:32.300
When they get lost on the way out, the group's
only female, Beverly, comes up with a strange

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.410
plan to help unite the group: she'll get busy
with them all, one by one.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.300
Immediately afterward, one of the boys remembers
how to escape the sewer system.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.710
Unsurprisingly, the scene was entirely dropped
for both the 2017 film and the 1990 miniseries.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.480
Screenwriter Gary Dauberman told Entertainment
Weekly:

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.379
"While it's an important scene, it doesn't
define the book in any way…

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.809
We know what the intent was of that scene
and why he put it in there, and we tried to

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:00.370
accomplish what the intent was in a different
way."

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.030
Me Before You

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:06.510
The romance film Me Before You had the benefit
of being adapted for the big screen by the

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:10.931
novel's author herself, Jojo Moyes…which
only makes it more interesting that the film

00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:13.150
lacks one rather critical plot point.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:17.690
In the movie, the female protagonist, Louisa
"Lou" Clark is quirky and intelligent, which

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.170
is why nobody really understands what keeps
her from spreading her wings.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.990
In the book, however, we know exactly what
Lou's deal is: in high school, she was assaulted—something

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.740
she keeps firmly under wraps.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.860
This critical plot point is used to explain
pretty much everything about Lou, from her

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.799
weird and overtly conservative sense of style
to her offbeat personality.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:41.020
Moyes claimed they tried to put that scene
into the movie, but it simply didn't fit,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.020
saying,

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.580
"I worked with the director a long time writing
this scene and trying different variations

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:46.580
of it.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.280
It achieves a different weight when you do
that in pictorial form.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.710
The more we tried, the more we realized it
just skewed the tone of the whole thing.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.140
It was changing the mood of the story."

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.509
The Road

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:03.620
Cormac McCarthy's 2006 Pulitzer-winning novel
The Road and its 2009 film adaptation tell

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:08.640
the story of a father and son's fight for
survival in a post-apocalyptic wasteland where

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.000
nearly all organic life has perished.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.419
The film has its fair share of heavy moments,
but one particularly disturbing scene was

00:02:15.419 --> 00:02:16.730
left by the roadside.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:21.260
In the book, the pair encounters a group of
survivors about to eat a baby—a scene which

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.930
was filmed but ultimately discarded.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.319
Director John Hillcoat explained:

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:27.519
"It all works in the book because it's in
your head.

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:30.959
When you visualize some of this stuff it just
becomes too much."

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:33.730
We'd have to agree with him on that.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:34.879
Paper Towns

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:40.840
Adapted from John Green's young adult novel
of the same name, 2015's mystery dramedy Paper

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.559
Towns tells a coming-of-age story about an
all-night road trip of revenge.

00:02:45.559 --> 00:02:49.549
However, one particular scene from the book
was intentionally left out of the film.

00:02:49.549 --> 00:02:53.790
In the book, the two main characters, Quentin
and Margo, break into SeaWorld at the end

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:54.790
of the night.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.949
However, following the release of the documentary
Blackfish—which shone a light on on what

00:02:58.949 --> 00:03:04.219
happens to whales in captivity—the screenwriters
decided to drop the SeaWorld part completely

00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:08.339
and have the characters end their night at
Orlando's SunTrust building instead.

00:03:08.339 --> 00:03:10.580
Producer Wyck Godfrey explained the change,
saying,

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.819
"Since [John] wrote the book, the documentary
[Blackfish] came out.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:17.480
I think it's a little less playful to go to
SeaWorld now."

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:18.829
Divergent

00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:24.049
In order to maintain a financially friendly
PG-13 rating, the film adaptation of Veronica

00:03:24.049 --> 00:03:29.260
Roth's post-apocalyptic YA novel Divergent
had to be toned down in a major way.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.609
But one particularly graphic scene from the
book was cut for different reasons altogether.

00:03:33.609 --> 00:03:38.559
In the scene, the Dauntless initiate played
by Ben Lamb wakes up with a knife in his eyeball.

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:42.260
Director Neil Burger explained that he'd shot
the scene, but then decided to leave it out

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.930
of the theatrical release because it was "interrupting
the flow of the story."

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:46.930
He told CinemaBlend:

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:50.439
"...even though it’s a good scene, that’s
really the art of trying to figure out where

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:54.519
the movie is working and sometimes what’s
slowing it down is one of your favorite scenes,

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:57.119
and you’ve got to cut it out."

00:03:57.119 --> 00:03:58.369
Gone with the Wind

00:03:58.369 --> 00:04:03.359
You can't talk about the history of American
cinema without mentioning Gone with the Wind.

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:07.749
Adapting the racially charged novel for the
big screen, however, was no easy feat.

00:04:07.749 --> 00:04:12.269
As The Atlantic points out, in the book, the
central black characters are "content with

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:13.900
slavery, uninterested in freedom."

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.689
Author Margaret Mitchell once describes a
character as having "the uncomprehending sadness

00:04:18.689 --> 00:04:20.030
of a monkey's face."

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:25.090
Even worse, the book calls the Ku Klux Klan
a, quote, "tragic necessity."

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.740
Obviously, the producers had a hard time remaining
faithful to the original story.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.280
Even for the 1930s, some of those scenes were
a little too racist.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.650
American Psycho

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:42.509
Mary Harron's film adaptation of Bret Easton
Ellis's 1991 bestseller American Psycho doesn't

00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:44.540
shy away from disturbing scenes.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:49.509
We watch the film's protagonist, Patrick Bateman,
brutally dispatch multiple people and fill

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:51.360
up a colleague's apartment with bodies.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.020
Still, the film doesn't even touch some of
the scenes from the book, which is so disturbing

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.129
that The New York Times said it was "understandable"
when Simon &amp; Schuster refused to publish it.

00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:05.139
It's some pretty messed up stuff, and Harron
probably made the right choice when she decided

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:09.590
to keep most of the brutality off the screen…
including one horrible chainsaw scene.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:14.039
On the plus side, at least we got Christian
Bale's moonwalking Huey Lewis monologue.

00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:19.009
"He's been compared to Elvis Costello, but
I think Huey has a far more bitter, cynical

00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:20.009
sense of humor."

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:21.009
Thanks for watching!

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:23.340
